CAT = "llqpDisability"; SEG = "DIS"
Q = [
("Under an own occupation definition of total disability, a surgeon who can no longer operate but can teach is:",
 "Considered totally disabled", ["Not disabled because she can teach", "Partially disabled only", "Disabled only if she stops teaching"],
 "Own occupation pays when the insured cannot perform the main duties of their own occupation, even if they can work in another one."),
("An any occupation definition pays only when the insured cannot work in:",
 "Any job suited to their training", ["Their own job at the time of claim", "A job paying the same salary", "Any job at all, of any kind"],
 "Any occupation means any occupation for which the insured is reasonably suited by education, training or experience. Many group plans switch to it after two years."),
("The elimination period in a disability policy is:",
 "The wait before benefits start", ["The time limit for filing a claim", "The months benefits are paid", "The years before premiums stop"],
 "Benefits begin only after the elimination (waiting) period. A longer period lowers the premium, and short-term savings or EI can bridge it."),
("Choosing a 120-day elimination period instead of a 30-day period will usually:",
 "Lower the premium", ["Raise the premium", "Lengthen the benefit period", "Remove the waiting period"],
 "The insurer pays fewer short claims when the waiting period is longer, so the premium drops."),
("A residual disability benefit pays when the insured:",
 "Returns to work at reduced income", ["Is fully disabled for the rest of life", "Dies during a period of disability", "Has a condition from before the policy"],
 "Residual benefits pay a share of the total disability benefit in proportion to the income lost, encouraging a gradual return to work."),
("A recurrent disability provision treats a relapse within a set time after recovery as:",
 "The same claim, with no new wait", ["A new claim with a new waiting period", "A pre-existing condition to exclude", "Grounds for the insurer to cancel"],
 "If the same cause recurs within the stated period (often six months), the claim continues as the original disability and the elimination period is not repeated."),
("A non-cancellable disability policy means the insurer cannot:",
 "Cancel it or raise its premium", ["Ever deny a claim under it", "Change the benefit period later", "Ask for any proof of disability"],
 "Non-cancellable and guaranteed renewable: the insurer must renew and cannot change the premium. Guaranteed renewable alone allows class-wide premium increases."),
("A guaranteed renewable disability policy lets the insurer:",
 "Raise premiums for a whole class", ["Cancel the policy after any claim", "Raise one person's premium alone", "Lower the benefits at each renewal"],
 "The policy must be renewed, but premiums can change for everyone in the same risk class. They cannot be raised for one insured because of their claims."),
("A cost of living adjustment rider on disability income:",
 "Raises benefits with inflation on claim", ["Lowers the premiums whenever prices rise", "Adds coverage before any claim", "Pays a lump sum at age 65"],
 "COLA increases the monthly benefit while the insured is on claim, usually tied to the consumer price index up to a cap."),
("A future income option rider on a disability policy allows the insured to:",
 "Add benefit as income grows, no medical", ["Retire early on full benefits", "Take all benefits as a lump sum", "Skip the elimination period on one claim"],
 "The rider lets the insured add monthly benefit on set dates in line with higher income, proving income but not health."),
("Why do insurers cap disability benefits at a share of pre-disability income?",
 "To keep an incentive to go back to work", ["Because tax law forbids paying any more", "Because EI always pays the rest", "To shorten the elimination period"],
 "If benefits matched or exceeded earnings, a claimant would have little reason to recover and return. Insurers issue limits based on earned income."),
("A presumptive disability clause treats the insured as totally disabled after:",
 "Loss of sight, speech or two limbs", ["Any hospital stay of one week", "Being laid off by an employer", "Two missed days of work in a row at the job"],
 "Presumptive disability pays full benefits for specified severe losses, even if the person keeps working."),
("Disability insurance that covers both accident and sickness is preferable to accident-only cover because:",
 "Most disabilities come from illness", ["Accidents are not insurable", "Sickness cover is always cheaper", "Accident cover has no waiting period"],
 "Illnesses such as back conditions, cancer and mental health conditions cause most long disabilities, so accident-only coverage leaves the biggest gap."),
("A disability policy has a benefit period to age 65. This means benefits:",
 "Can be paid to 65 while disabled", ["Start only once the insured is 65", "Last 65 months at the very most", "Stop after any return to work"],
 "The benefit period is how long benefits can be paid for one disability: here, as long as the insured stays disabled up to age 65."),
("A rehabilitation benefit in a disability policy is designed to:",
 "Pay for training to return to work", ["Replace lost income in full", "Refund the premiums once the insured recovers", "Cover hospital room charges"],
 "Insurers fund vocational or physical rehabilitation because helping the insured back to work shortens claims."),
("A self-employed graphic designer buys individual disability insurance. How are her benefits taxed if she pays the premiums herself?",
 "They are tax-free", ["They are taxed as income", "Half is taxed", "They are taxed as a capital gain"],
 "Benefits from an individual disability policy paid for personally with after-tax money are received tax-free. Her premiums are not deductible."),
("A business overhead expense policy pays the business's fixed costs during the owner's disability. Its premiums are:",
 "Deductible, and benefits taxable", ["Not deductible, benefits tax-free", "Deductible, and benefits tax-free", "Not deductible, benefits taxable"],
 "Overhead expense premiums are a business expense, so the benefits that replace those expenses are taxable to the business."),
("A disability buy-out policy is used to:",
 "Buy out a disabled owner's shares", ["Pay the disabled partner's salary", "Cover the firm's office rent", "Repay the owner's home mortgage"],
 "It pays a lump sum or instalments so healthy owners can buy the disabled owner's interest under the buy-sell agreement, usually after a long waiting period."),
("A disability policy excludes a back condition the applicant was treated for last year. This is an example of:",
 "An exclusion rider", ["A waiver of premium", "A residual benefit", "A recurrent disability clause"],
 "Underwriting can issue coverage but exclude a specific condition through an exclusion rider, rather than declining or rating the whole policy."),
("Kofi is disabled and receives benefits from an individual policy. Under a waiver of premium provision, his premiums:",
 "Are waived while he is disabled", ["Are added to his monthly benefit", "Double while he is on claim", "Are paid for him by EI"],
 "Most disability policies waive premiums after the insured has been disabled for a set period and while the disability continues."),
]
